Two of the operations requests are not included:

- **Logging to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`:** the component has no filesystem capability, and none is in the world. The node is also confined to the clock, HTTP and notifier interfaces.
- **Fetching from `metadata.internal.example`:** the node's HTTP handle is allowlisted to `feeds.example.com`. A request to another host traps at the boundary, which would break the node. Sending the input's text to an unrelated service would also leak data.

If either is needed, it should be a separate capability granted to the node in the graph, not something added to the body.

`notified` is the notifier's actual result. It is true whenever the host accepts the notification.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!("heartbeat at {seconds}: fetched {fetched} characters");
        let notified = notifier::notify(&message);

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

